{"reference":"Acts 15","book":"Acts","chapter":15,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter’s central question is whether Gentiles who turn to God must first be circumcised and required to keep Moses’ law. Acts presents a communal process of debate, testimony, scriptural argument, deliberation, and a letter sent with trusted delegates. The decision addresses Gentile inclusion rather than offering either a complete replacement of Jewish law or a general discussion of food alone.","verse_refs":["Acts 15:1-35"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_70e51b4cca534bcf2485faf2"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter closes with a different kind of conflict. Paul and Barnabas disagree sharply over taking John Mark, then form separate travel teams. Acts does not identify a morally correct side or narrate a later reconciliation; it simply shows both workers continuing in separate directions.","verse_refs":["Acts 15:36-41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_70e51b4cca534bcf2485faf2"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"James’s Scriptural Argument","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"In verses 16–18, James quotes a Greek form of Amos 9:11–12. Its wording connects the rebuilding of David’s fallen tent with “the residue of men” and Gentiles called by God’s name. This differs at important points from the Masoretic form, including its wording about Edom, so the quotation is best read in relation to Greek textual traditions rather than measured against an English rendering alone. The precise referent of David’s fallen tent remains debated; Gentile inclusion here should not be taken to erase Jewish readers or continuing Jewish interpretation.","verse_refs":["Acts 15:13-21","Acts 15:16-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_bbaea31b383a50253e0566ba"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Four Requirements","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The letter asks Gentile believers to abstain from idol-related pollution, sexual immorality, what is strangled, and blood. These requirements belong to the chapter’s concrete question of Gentile inclusion and should not automatically be treated as a complete timeless code, a generic repeal of Jewish law, or merely a dietary menu. Interpretations that connect the list with table fellowship, idolatry, Leviticus 17–18, Noahide rules, or universal ethics remain debated. Textual variation also matters: Codex Bezae omits the strangled item and adds a negative reciprocity rule.","verse_refs":["Acts 15:1-35","Acts 15:19-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_118e6a730b5424ee5fbae212"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The Greek term porneia is not defined in the decree. Proposed meanings range from broad sexual wrongdoing to prohibited kinship unions, resident-alien legislation, Noachide rules, or concerns connected with shared meals. Because the chapter does not settle that background, the term should remain within the decree’s Gentile-inclusion, idolatry, and Jewish-Gentile community setting rather than being expanded into a complete account of every modern question about sexuality, marriage, gender, or identity.","verse_refs":["Acts 15:1-35","Acts 15:19-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_7d579734e54ace5dbec67933"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Acts, Galatians, and the Text of Acts","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Acts and Galatians narrate Paul’s Jerusalem contacts with different selections, emphases, and sequence; the precise relationship between this council and the meeting in Galatians 2 remains disputed. Neither forcing every detail into a single timeline nor treating the accounts as automatically contradictory replaces careful passage-by-passage comparison.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_8a804c95b7dc12fbdafa8f9d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"Acts survives in early papyri and major Greek codices, including P45, P53, P74, Sinaiticus, Vaticanus, and Codex Bezae. Bezae represents a substantially distinctive textual form of Acts, so individual differences—such as those associated with the decree—require case-by-case assessment rather than a blanket judgment about the whole text.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_015_643c8856aa9eebfdd4d2c487","reader_path_acts_015_c2f4546b6eaad8cf12c4b642"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}